6th Lecture - In class Notes:

- I have opened up python_functions_coding_style jypter notebook and am following in class. We are starting at the functions section.

- It is good practice to put a .after your numbers unless you specifically want them to be integers. This allows them to be run in different languages/versions.  

- Doc strings should have:
	- Input and outputs
	- Summary - one line summary that does not use variable names or the functions name
	- Explanation - this can be a few sentences or an even a paragraph. refer to variables using back-ticks like 'var'
	- Parameters - write what each variable is. Ex) var1: array_like , var2: int
	- Date Written and if updates were added (like more examples)

- All libraries needed need to be imported at the beginning of your code. Use these abbreviations:
 	- import bumpy as np
	- import scipy as sp
	- import matplotlib as mpl
	- import matplotlib.pyplot as put
	- import astropy.io.fits as fits

- When defining functions:
	- Leave spaces between commons
	- Do not leave spaces in between parameters or operations

- Most important thing in research is reproducibility so make sure to put the units and sources of values in comment

