Practicum 3:
	 Problem 1:
		(Part A): I read the data from practicum3_1.dat, which contained two data sets. I separated the data into Model 1 and Model 2. I then plotted the Model 1 data. Model 1 was generated from the function (f(x)=3.2x+1.2) with random noise, so the plotted data showed an approximately linear relationship.

		(Part B): I used the provided linfit() routine to fit a linear model to the Model 1 data. I initially received a ModuleNotFoundError because Python could not find linfit.py, so I made the provided linfit.py file accessible from my Practicum 3 working directory. I first performed the fit using the stated uncertainty of sigma_y = 0.5. I obtained an intercept of 1.300 +/- 0.100 and a slope of 3.185 +/- 0.018. The intercept differed from the true value of 1.2 by 0.998 sigma, and the slope differed from the true value of 3.2 by 0.865 sigma. Therefore, both fitted parameters were within 3 sigma of the true parameters.
I then repeated the fit using sigma_y = 0.2 and sigma_y = 0.9. For sigma_y = 0.2, I obtained an intercept of 1.300 +/- 0.040 and a slope of 3.185 +/- 0.007. For sigma_y = 0.9, I obtained an intercept of 1.300 +/- 0.180 and a slope of 3.185 +/- 0.032. The fitted slope and intercept remained the same because all of the data points were assigned the same uncertainty. However, increasing sigma_y increased the uncertainties of the fitted parameters.

		(Part C): I used the Model 1 linear fit with the stated uncertainty of sigma_y = 0.5 and examined the chi-squared value and probability returned by linfit(). The probability was approximately 0.84, or 84%. This means there was approximately an 84% probability of obtaining a higher chi-squared, or a worse fit, by chance if the data came from the fitted model with the stated random error. I then plotted the Model 1 data with error bars and the fitted linear model. The fitted line followed the data closely, which was expected because Model 1 was generated from a linear relationship. I added appropriate x- and y-axis labels, a title, and a legend. I saved the plot as practicum3_jfern_problem1_graph1.png

		(Part D): I repeated the linear fitting procedure using the second data set. Model 2 was generated from the quadratic function f(x) = 3.2x^2 + 1.2 with random noise. I used sigma_y = 0.5 and attempted to fit the data with a linear model. The linear fit produced an intercept of -50.560 +/- 0.100, a slope of 31.571 +/- 0.018, a chi-squared value of 240064.442, and a probability that was essentially zero. I plotted the Model 2 data with the linear fit and saved the graph as practicum3_jfern_problem1_graph2.png.

The linear model did not fit the Model 2 data well for two reasons. First, the data had a clearly curved shape while the linear model was a straight line. The data points showed systematic differences from the fitted line rather than random scatter around it. Second, the chi-squared value was extremely large and the probability of obtaining a worse fit by chance was essentially zero. These results showed that a linear model was not appropriate for the Model 2 data. The data could be fit more appropriately using a quadratic model because Model 2 follows a quadratic relationship.

	
	Problem 2:
		(Part A): I created an array containing 400 elements. I generated the first 396 elements using a Poisson distribution with an expected number of N = 10000 photons. I generated the final four elements using a uniform distribution between 0 and 10^6. These four values represented the 1% bad pixels in the CCD sample. I combined the Poisson and uniform data into one 400-element array. I then calculated the mean and median of the sample. I obtained a mean of 13392.36 and a median of 10009.0. The median was much closer to the expected value of N = 10000. The four bad pixels could contain very large values from the uniform distribution, which pulled the mean upward. The median was much less affected by these extreme outliers, which is why it remained close to the expected number of photons.

		(Part B): I calculated the standard deviation of the original 400-element sample and obtained 40199.54. I then performed sigma clipping by creating a mask that retained only data points that were within 5 sigma of the median. I used this mask to create a new clipped sample. After sigma clipping, I calculated the mean, median, and standard deviation of the new sample. The clipped mean was 10185.44, the clipped median was 10009.0, and the clipped standard deviation was 3489.94. The mean decreased from 13392.36 to 10185.44 and moved much closer to the expected value of N = 10000. The median remained at 10009.0 because it was less affected by the outliers. The standard deviation decreased from 40199.54 to 3489.94 because extreme values were removed from the sample. The clipped standard deviation was still relatively large because the original standard deviation was very large, which made the initial 5-sigma range very wide. Therefore, some bad pixels could remain after this single round of sigma clipping. Overall, the sigma clipping reduced the influence of the extreme outliers and caused the sample statistics to become more representative of the expected Poisson data.

